MWANGAZA HARDWARE

OPERATIONS AND MAINTENANCE STANDARD

Document: "OPERATIONS_AND_MAINTENANCE.md"
Project: Mwangaza Hardware website and digital services
Status: Proposed for approval and adoption
Version: 1.0.0
Governing document: "PROJECT_CONSTITUTION.md"

1. PURPOSE

This document defines how the website will be operated, monitored, maintained, recovered and transferred without depending on a developer for routine tasks.

The operating model is:

- Project owner: approves business decisions, spending, access and releases.
- ChatGPT: assists with diagnosis, code changes, test design, documentation and recovery instructions.
- GitHub: preserves source history, change records, automated checks and deployment workflows.
- Hosting and service providers: provide infrastructure, status information, backups and account-level recovery capabilities as supported.

No person or tool may claim an issue is resolved without evidence.

2. ACCOUNT OWNERSHIP AND ACCESS

2.1 Required ownership

The authorized business owner must control, or have documented administrative access to:

- Domain registration.
- DNS configuration.
- Hosting and deployment.
- GitHub repository and organization, where applicable.
- Business email and account recovery methods.
- Analytics and search-console accounts.
- WhatsApp Business and other official social accounts.
- Payment-provider merchant accounts when introduced.
- Database and backup services when introduced.

The marketing agency must not be the sole holder of an essential account or recovery method.

2.2 Account protection

- Use unique passwords and a password manager.
- Enable multifactor authentication wherever available.
- Keep recovery codes in a secure location outside the repository.
- Review account access periodically and after personnel changes.
- Remove access that is no longer necessary.
- Never share one-time verification codes in chat.
- Never commit secrets or recovery codes to GitHub.

2.3 Renewal and billing records

Maintain a private register of each service, account owner, renewal date, billing method, cost, recovery route and cancellation process.

Do not store passwords, payment-card details or secret keys in that register.

Set renewal reminders sufficiently early to prevent domain, hosting or business-email expiration.

3. SINGLE POINTS OF FAILURE

The project must avoid depending on one person, one account, one provider or one undocumented procedure.

At minimum:

- Keep source code in version control.
- Maintain secure account-recovery arrangements.
- Document how to deploy the last known-good release.
- Keep independent backups of important business data.
- Document how to export data from essential services.
- Maintain alternative customer-contact methods.
- Ensure the calculator and educational content do not depend on AI availability.
- Document what happens if the hosting provider or messaging provider is unavailable.

Where a service cannot be made redundant economically, document the risk, its impact and the recovery plan.

4. WEBSITE AVAILABILITY MONITORING

4.1 Required checks

Use an appropriate uptime monitor or hosting-provider health check to test the live website at a reasonable interval.

Monitor, where technically supported:

- Website availability.
- Critical page responses.
- Deployment failures.
- Application errors.
- Failed enquiry submissions.
- Database connectivity.
- Storage or service limits.
- External API failures.
- Payment notification failures when payments are introduced.

Monitoring must not expose private customer data.

4.2 Alerts

Choose an alert destination controlled by the authorized owner.

Define:

- Which events generate alerts.
- Who responds.
- How urgent failures are prioritized.
- How to acknowledge and close an incident.
- What to do if the alerting service itself fails.

A green uptime indicator does not prove that the calculator, enquiry form or payment workflow works. Important user journeys require separate checks.

5. NO-SILENT-FAILURE RULE

Every important operation must have a detectable outcome.

Examples:

- An enquiry must be confirmed only after the server or service confirms successful receipt.
- A failed enquiry must display a useful error and provide an alternative contact method.
- A payment must not be marked successful merely because a user returned to the website.
- A failed backup must generate a detectable warning.
- A deployment must be verified against the intended commit.
- A failed AI request must fall back to normal help information.

Logs should record enough technical context to diagnose failures without unnecessarily storing personal information, passwords, tokens or payment details.

6. ENQUIRY AND EMAIL OPERATIONS

Before the public launch:

1. Submit test enquiries from a real phone.
2. Verify that the intended recipient receives them.
3. Check spam and junk folders.
4. Confirm that visitors receive accurate success or failure feedback.
5. Test invalid input and missing fields.
6. Test duplicate submissions and interrupted connections.
7. Verify that failed delivery can be detected.
8. Document an alternative contact method.

If the website sends email from its own domain, configure appropriate sender authentication, including SPF, DKIM and DMARC where applicable to the chosen mail setup.

Use rate limits, input validation and appropriate spam controls. Do not expose internal recipient addresses unnecessarily or publish private enquiry records.

7. CONTENT AND PRODUCT MANAGEMENT

Every product and business fact must have a reliable source.

Maintain a controlled process for updating:

- Product names and categories.
- Prices and units.
- Product images and descriptions.
- Stock or availability information.
- Discounts and delivery charges.
- Contact details.
- DIY articles.
- FAQs and policies.
- Marketing promotions.

Content update procedure

1. Identify the information that needs updating.
2. Confirm the new information with an authorized source.
3. Make the smallest relevant change.
4. Check spelling, links, units and calculations.
5. Preview the change on mobile.
6. Publish through the approved workflow.
7. Confirm that the live page displays the intended information.
8. Record important changes where appropriate.

Never invent prices or display unconfirmed stock availability.

If a price update affects a quotation or estimate, follow the approved rules for refreshing existing calculations and identifying outdated estimates.

8. BACKUPS AND DATA EXPORTS

8.1 Data requiring protection

Depending on the features actually deployed, protect:

- Source code and configuration templates.
- Product catalogue.
- Product images and uploaded documents.
- Enquiries and customer records.
- Orders and quotations.
- Payment and reconciliation records.
- Commission calculations and approvals.
- Relevant database structure and migration history.

Keep secrets separate from ordinary backups and protect backup access.

8.2 Backup policy

For each data store, document:

- What is backed up.
- Backup frequency.
- Retention period.
- Storage location.
- Encryption and access protection.
- Who can restore it.
- Expected recovery time.
- Acceptable data-loss window.

Choose backup frequency according to the importance and change rate of the data. Financial records and live orders require stricter protection than replaceable demonstration content.

8.3 Restore testing

A backup is not verified until a restoration test succeeds.

- Restore into a safe, separate environment.
- Confirm that the required records and files are present.
- Check data consistency.
- Verify that the restored application can use the data.
- Record the test date, result and issues.
- Correct failed restore procedures before relying on them.

Do not overwrite current production data during a test restore.

9. DEPENDENCY AND SECURITY MAINTENANCE

On a regular schedule:

- Review dependency vulnerability alerts.
- Check for abandoned or unsupported packages.
- Update dependencies in small, reviewable groups.
- Run the full applicable test suite after updates.
- Review account access and permissions.
- Check for accidental secret exposure.
- Review hosting and framework security notices.
- Verify that backup and restore procedures remain valid.

Do not blindly apply major dependency upgrades to production.

For every meaningful update, preserve the previous known-good version and verify the build, key user journeys and rollback method.

10. PERFORMANCE AND MOBILE MAINTENANCE

Periodically test the website on representative Android devices and mobile browsers.

Check:

- Page loading speed.
- Layout stability.
- Catalogue search and filtering.
- Calculator responsiveness.
- Form submission.
- Image loading.
- Navigation and tap targets.
- Error messages.
- Accessibility.
- Behavior on slow or interrupted connections.

Use repeatable measurements where possible and compare results with earlier measurements.

Investigate significant regressions rather than assuming that a successful deployment means acceptable performance.

11. DOMAIN, HOSTING AND PROVIDER PORTABILITY

Maintain documented export and migration procedures for essential services.

The project must know how to:

- Export source code.
- Export the product catalogue and business records.
- Export database data where applicable.
- Preserve uploaded images and documents.
- Recreate hosting and environment configuration.
- Update DNS safely.
- Restore the previous deployment.
- Move to an alternative provider if necessary.

Before adopting a service, assess its export facilities, lock-in risks, recurring fees and exit procedure.

Never assume that deleting an account or switching providers preserves the underlying data.

12. INCIDENT SEVERITY AND RESPONSE

Critical

Examples include exposed credentials, unauthorized access, suspected serious data breaches, incorrect payment handling or corruption of financial records.

Actions:

1. Stop affected automated operations where safe.
2. Restrict access or revoke compromised credentials.
3. Preserve relevant logs and evidence.
4. Notify the authorized owner immediately.
5. Follow applicable legal and provider notification obligations.
6. Restore only through a verified process.
7. Require explicit review before resuming affected financial or sensitive operations.

High

Examples include the website being unavailable, all enquiries failing or a major user journey being broken.

Actions:

1. Confirm the impact.
2. Inspect provider status, logs and recent deployments.
3. Assess whether rollback is safer than a new fix.
4. Restore service using a verified procedure.
5. Test the affected user journey.
6. Record the cause and prevention measure.

Normal

Examples include minor layout defects, outdated content or a noncritical broken link.

Actions:

1. Record the defect.
2. Prioritize it.
3. Make a small correction.
4. Run the relevant tests.
5. Verify the result and document the change.

13. SELF-SERVICE REPAIR PROCEDURE

For every defect, follow this sequence:

1. Capture the exact error, screenshot, URL or failed workflow log.
2. Identify when the problem started.
3. Check the most recent change or deployment.
4. Consult the relevant project instructions.
5. Ask ChatGPT to explain the evidence and propose a testable diagnosis.
6. Preserve affected data or configuration before risky changes.
7. Make one controlled change.
8. Run the relevant tests and regression checks.
9. Verify the fix in preview or staging where available.
10. Deploy only after the applicable gates pass.
11. Confirm the result on the live website.
12. Record the cause, fix and any remaining risk.

Do not repeat speculative edits when the evidence does not support the proposed cause.

For complex incidents, payment discrepancies, serious security issues or unrecoverable data problems, stop and involve the relevant provider or qualified specialist where necessary.

14. RELEASE AND ROLLBACK OPERATIONS

Before each release:

- Confirm the approved source revision.
- Confirm automated checks pass.
- Verify the production configuration.
- Check relevant backups and database migrations.
- Identify the last known-good release.
- Confirm the rollback procedure.
- Complete the applicable release checklist.

After deployment:

- Confirm the intended version is live.
- Check the home page and affected routes.
- Test the changed user journey.
- Inspect relevant errors and logs.
- Confirm important forms and integrations work.
- Monitor for regressions.

If a release causes a serious problem, assess rollback promptly. A rollback must not destroy newer legitimate orders or financial records.

15. ROUTINE MAINTENANCE SCHEDULE

After every code change

- Run applicable automated tests.
- Review security and functionality impacts.
- Confirm the relevant user journey.
- Record the change and result.

Weekly

- Review uptime and error reports.
- Check enquiry delivery.
- Inspect unresolved issues and dependency alerts.
- Verify important contact links.
- Review hosting usage and unusual cost increases.

Monthly

- Review account access and spending.
- Check domain and service renewal dates.
- Review mobile performance and accessibility.
- Inspect product and policy accuracy.
- Check backup completion.
- Review privacy and security alerts.
- Review analytics and enquiry trends.

Periodically, based on risk

- Perform a restore test.
- Test incident and rollback procedures.
- Review provider portability.
- Review the security configuration.
- Reassess the service budget and architecture.

The owner must set actual dates and assign responsibility. This schedule is a proposed minimum operating routine, not evidence that the checks have already been configured.

16. COST AND SERVICE CONTROL

Before enabling a paid service, document:

- Setup cost.
- Recurring cost.
- Usage-based charges.
- Expected monthly volume and cost.
- Available spending limits and alerts.
- Cancellation procedure.
- Export and migration options.
- A lower-cost alternative.

No paid integration may be activated without the required approval.

Where possible, preserve core browsing, educational content and calculator functionality when AI, messaging, analytics or other optional services are unavailable.

17. RECORDS AND ACCOUNTABILITY

Maintain a record of:

- Significant releases.
- Incidents and their causes.
- Recovery actions.
- Backup and restore tests.
- Important configuration changes.
- Security fixes.
- Provider and billing changes.
- Approved exceptions.
- Unresolved operational risks.

Do not store passwords, private keys, card security codes or unnecessary personal information in these records.

18. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

This operating standard is implemented only when the relevant controls have been configured and verified.

Acceptance requires evidence that:

- Essential account ownership and recovery methods are documented.
- Monitoring and alert destinations are configured where applicable.
- Enquiry delivery has been tested.
- Backup and restore procedures exist for each critical data store.
- A safe deployment and rollback procedure has been tested.
- Dependency and security review processes are defined.
- Product updates have a controlled workflow.
- Incident response instructions are accessible.
- Data-export and provider-exit procedures are documented.
- Routine maintenance responsibilities are assigned.

Any control that cannot yet be implemented must be recorded as a limitation with an owner and a planned resolution.

Final rule: Routine operations must be understandable and repeatable by the project owner using ChatGPT, GitHub and documented provider controls. Every important repair must have evidence. Where a problem exceeds the safe capabilities of those tools, the system must identify the limitation instead of concealing it.
